<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<pages:BinderPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             xmlns:pages="clr-namespace:Foreword.Android.Pages"
             x:Class="Foreword.Android.Pages.CalendarsPage"
             Title="Calendars">
    <!--
        C1 in the mockups: which calendar feeds which column.

        THIS SCREEN REPLACES A JSON EDITOR. The mapping was a mono Editor on the Setup page where
        the owner hand-typed CalendarMappingOptions as JSON — opaque Google ids paired with column
        names, no validation, and no way to know what you had. This is that, as a list, with the
        real names: IGoogleCalendarClient.ListCalendars() already returned id to summary and NOTHING
        HAS EVER CALLED IT. It was written for this screen and never wired to one, so the names cost
        no new API work.

        THERE IS NO REFRESH BUTTON. The calendar list is re-read every morning anyway, so a manual
        one would usually show the same list — the kind of button Score's settings refuses on
        principle. What a new calendar gets instead is the "Arrived since you last looked" group,
        which needs nothing pressed.

        Mockups: https://claude.ai/code/artifact/6bb65b0d-04da-47f3-bf72-a04020217e0c
    -->
    <ScrollView>
        <VerticalStackLayout Padding="{StaticResource ScreenPadding}" Spacing="0">


            <Label x:Name="StatusLabel" Style="{StaticResource StatusLine}" Margin="0,8,0,0" />

            <!-- The groups are built in code: how many calendars there are, and which of them are
                 new, is not known until Google answers. -->
            <VerticalStackLayout x:Name="Groups" />

            <Label Text="Names come from Google — you never type an id."
                   Style="{StaticResource CardCaption}" />

        </VerticalStackLayout>
    </ScrollView>
</pages:BinderPage>
